        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Expect seven to ten years from a sump pump, and expect the switch, not the motor, to be the most likely reason it fails to start. A pump that runs constantly, sits in a dirty pit or short cycles will reach the end sooner, while a cast iron pump in a clean pit that rarely runs can go longer.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Because a sump pump fails when you need it most, treat it as scheduled maintenance. Test it with water every few months, add a water alarm and a backup if your basement matters to you, and replace the pump around year seven in a finished or wet basement rather than waiting for the storm that proves it was time.
          </p>
